WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.860 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Silicon Valley is the new American dream  it's where companies are born and  millionaires are made like many of you I  was part of that dream before coming to  the GSB I was an executive at Cisco and  then went on to work for a  venture-backed startup what I'd like to  start with a much simpler dream the  dream of my grandparents I'm Sonia  Nicola they dreamt of running water  electricity and indoor plumbing things  they didn't have in their small village  in Mexico so they moved to the United  States in search of a better life where  they became migrant farm workers who  harvested the fields right here in  Silicon Valley before it became Google  Apple and Facebook but as you know the  field disappeared and when that happened  my family transitioned to role is to  provide the backbone of this valley the  people who clean your homes the people  who take out your trash and the people  who even take care of your children one  morning before school I got up really  early to go with my grandparents to work  I walked into the bathroom to help my  grandmother clean and I had never seen  such a mess before my grandmother saw  the look on my face and she explained  how important this job was but it would  allow us to put food on the table she  encouraged me to take pride in my work  to make that bathroom a sparkling clean  and I could make it my grandmother who  had a fourth-grade education taught me  that day to approach each and every task  with dignity and with grit in Silicon  Valley venture capitalists are in search  of the next billion dollar startup the  elusive unicorn will people from my  neighborhood we have a very different  definition of what a unicorn is I grew  up in East San Jose  about 20 miles south of here when I  think of home I think of my  grandmother's yellow kitchen I think of  the scent of her homemade tortillas and  I think of the sounds of money action  music as a little girl I love to play  outside and one day I meant to sneak out  when no one was looking and was happily  playing in the garden when I was a  corner of my eye I saw my grandmother  running towards me she scooped me into  her arms and like many Mexican  grandmothers scolded me in Spanish and  then made the sign of the Cross you see  my grandmother knew that although our  neighborhood was very beautiful it could  also be very dangerous  it meant walking home from school and  having a gun pulled on you it meant  being peer pressured to join a gang it  meant having a baby when you were a baby  my family wasn't immune to the sadly and  of my 20 cousins only 10 graduated from  high school and only three of us would  go on to graduate from college this  isn't unique to my family what is  happening to Latino families across the  country Latinos with college degrees  these are the unicorns in Silicon Valley  technology has been booming all around  us it's been creating tremendous  opportunity and wealth it's been  exciting and my family's been able to  take part of a little piece of it  my younger brother of equal is one of  the smartest guys I know it would really  frustrate me when I was stuck on my  physics homework assignment and he would  manage to solve it effortlessly he's the  kind of guy who loved to take things  apart and put them back together  he was a natural engineer my brother is  also a high school dropout and he became  a dad when he was seven  years old my brother was able to turn  his wife around to go back to school and  find a job working in manufacturing for  a local tech company because of that  opportunity he was able not only to  provide for his family but also for me  and one summer my brother gave me his  paychecks so that I could live in Paris  and study at the Sorbonne II that simple  act of generosity and love put me on a  different track it's the reason I'm here  with all of you but even with that  college degree my path to a career in  technology was very different than the  path that many of you took it was the  hustle I worked at three different  temporary agencies before I landed that  first job at Cisco and I will never  forget receiving that offer letter and  being so excited not only what it meant  for me but what it went for meant for my  family but the bubble burst the  manufacturing jobs went away my brother  lost to the job other people my family  lost their jobs my parents couldn't  afford their home anymore so they look  to me the one that's a good job to help  that entry-level salary at Cisco barely  covered a third of their mortgage so my  parents moved to central California back  to the fields where it all began  the Silicon Valley dream it was out of  reach for them while this area has been  growing at record pace communities like  East San Jose have been suffering one  setback after the other loss of jobs  loss of homes and lack of upward  mobility this isn't just my problem it's  our problem  Latinos represent one of the  fastest-growing groups in the country  this is expected to project a 1.5  trillion dollar opportunity think about  that I bet you didn't know that Latinos  have this beautiful entrepreneurial  spirit they start businesses at record  rate within my family alone there been  five businesses that have been started  so why if Latinos are starting  businesses at record rate why aren't we  able to capitalize on them  professor Jerry Porras here at the GSB  has been studying this very phenomenon  as part of the Stanford Latino  entrepreneurship initiative and one of  the things that he's discovered is  Latinos lack access to mentors as one of  the few people from my neighborhood who  had a career in technology I knew how  important it was to go back so I went to  the schools I attended and volunteered I  hosted events at local libraries I even  started a small scholarship fund through  this work I met Carlos Carlos had just  finished his freshman year at Harvard  when I asked Carlos Woods plans were for  the summer he looked at me and said that  he was going to work construction with  his dad when I asked Carlos have you  thought of an internship he said  Suchi I would love an internship but I  have no idea where to begin  Carlos was one of the lucky ones I one  of the leading institutions in this  country and yet even he was struggling  to navigate this business landscape I  knew how that felt but more importantly  I know how to help them find that  internship people like Carlos may not  have the polished resume they may not  have the pedigree but what they do have  is dignity grit and that entrepreneurial  spirit what they're missing is  opportunity so what I'd like to ask each  of you is that  you either are or will become leaders in  business in this valley and across the  world please look again at people from a  different path hire them mentor them  imagine the impact that all of us could  have on communities like East San Jose  if we add opportunity to the equation  [Applause]  [Music]

